00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.540
put them into the Dory.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.090
I'll answer some
of them at the end.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.210
But I will make sure that
I answer every question.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.930
If not in house today, then
I'll answer them online.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.069
So keep the link
to the Dory itself.

00:00:55.069 --> 00:00:56.860
The instructions are
here on how to use it.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:57.750
You use the app.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.249
And you can see the links that
you're going to follow there.

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:02.920
And then you put your
questions into the Dory Q&amp;A.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.650
We actually had a
couple of questions

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.650
about an hour ago before
the session even began,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.270
which I thought was really cool.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.640
So thank you, whoever it was
that asked those questions.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.721
And I've answered them already.

00:01:12.721 --> 00:01:13.220
OK.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.240
So we're going to talk
about machine learning.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.750
And there's this ongoing
revolution that's

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.130
happening in machine learning.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.610
And because of TensorFlow,
and because of how

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.230
we've open-sourced TensorFlow,
the goal behind that

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.420
is that you can be a
part of that revolution.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.610
This isn't something
that we're just doing.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.050
We've open-sourced
this platform.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.425
We've worked with
lots of people.

00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:33.410
We've worked with
lots of partners

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.660
to build some really
interesting scenarios.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.400
But any one of these scenarios
is something that you can do.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.299
And you're not going to
require great expertise

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:43.340
in machine learning or AI
or any of these things.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.070
We're democratizing it to
make it easy for everybody.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.110
But a couple of my favorite
examples that I want to share--

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.656
so first of all, I actually
studied physics in university.

00:01:53.656 --> 00:01:55.280
And back when I was
going to university

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.760
was when they discovered the
first extrasolar planets.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.250
And how the first extrasolar
planets were discovered

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.867
were by some of the best
astrophysicists in the world.

00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:05.450
And what they were
doing was that they

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.520
were taking a look at stars.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.550
And some stars-- very
distant, very large stars--

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.075
would have a slight
wobble in them.

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:14.200
And from time to time they
would see this star just

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.124
do a little wobble.

00:02:15.124 --> 00:02:17.540
And they tried to figure out
what was causing that wobble.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.165
And then they theorized, what if
there was a really big planet,

00:02:20.165 --> 00:02:23.751
like a Jupiter or a Saturn,
orbiting close to that star?

00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:26.000
Then what would happen is
the gravitational attraction

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.880
of the large massed planet
could cause a wobble in the star

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:29.720
itself.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.350
And as a result we discovered
the first extrasolar planets.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.600
But they were very
large planets that

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.990
were very close to
their parent stars.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.550
In the 20-odd years
since then we've

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.790
discovered thousands and
thousands of planets.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.170
And just in the last year,
through machine learning

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:49.520
and through machines being
able to spot patterns

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.590
in the behavior
of stars, not only

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.320
are we discovering
extrasolar planets,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.500
but we're actually able to see
things like in the Kepler-90

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.190
system.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.010
We're able to count the
planets that are in the star.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:04.730
And the Kepler-90i-- this
planet is a small, rocky planet

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.880
which has got an orbital
period of only about 14 days.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.320
So it's no longer limited to
large planets that are really

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.580
close to their host star.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.360
We're actually being able
to map these solar systems.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.157
And in the case of
the Kepler-90 system,

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:20.990
we've actually mapped
eight planets and what

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.790
the size of those
planets are, and then

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.650
through spectrographic
analysis actually

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.470
been able to identify
some of the materials

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.910
and the makeup of these planets.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.480
Another example is in something
called diabetic retinopathy.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.870
And diabetic retinopathy
is a very common disease.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.700
And it can cause
blindness and worse

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.320
if it's not discovered early.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.360
And if it's discovered
early, it's very cheap,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.462
and it's very easy
to treat with drugs.

00:03:43.462 --> 00:03:45.170
And some of the machine
learning research

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.090
that we've been working
on has actually,

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.580
through just simple
pictures of the cornea,

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.560
been able to spot
some of the markers

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.180
of diabetic retinopathy.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.830
So it can be diagnosed early.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.250
And it can be diagnosed
really cheaply.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.690
And now we're lucky
enough, most of us,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.130
to live in places with great
hospitals like San Francisco--

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.070
I'm from Seattle-- and
that kind of thing.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.570
But there are parts of the world
that they don't have access

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.327
to this kind of
imaging equipment.

00:04:09.327 --> 00:04:10.910
And we think, wouldn't
it be marvelous

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.800
that if someday and
someday very soon just

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:14.820
something cheap
like a cell phone--

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.839
that you will be able to do
the machine inference on a cell

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:20.300
phone to be able to take
a photo of a cornea,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.075
load it into a cell phone,
and do a diagnosis right away.

00:04:23.075 --> 00:04:24.950
Things like this we're
learning are beginning

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:26.750
to revolutionize health care.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.909
And we've found some
of our results--

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:32.450
while some of the most skillful
ophthalmologists in the world

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.370
have like a 99%
hit rate in being

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.380
able to diagnose these
things, some machine

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.630
learning models that
we've been building

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.932
are actually doing the same.

00:04:39.932 --> 00:04:42.140
They're equivalent and
sometimes even slightly better

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.790
than a human ophthalmologist.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.800
Now, the idea here is not
to replace ophthalmologists.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.980
The idea is to enhance
what they can do.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.650
If an ophthalmologist can see
100 people in a day instead

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.320
of five people in a day by
using something like this,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.512
everybody benefits.

00:04:56.512 --> 00:04:58.470
Another one that I've
been really interested in

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.070
and I find fascinating
is an understanding

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.320
and being able to control the
deforestation of our planet.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.790
And there have been some
machine-learned projects that

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.550
have been built where
what they've done--

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.630
I believe it was in
the Amazon rain forest

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.889
that they used old
Android phones.

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:16.430
You know those ones that you're
probably going to throw away?

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.929
And they've been
building TensorFlow

00:05:17.929 --> 00:05:21.536
machine-learned models that
will spot the signs of logging.

00:05:21.536 --> 00:05:22.910
And spotting the
signs of logging

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.240
can actually be very simple.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.670
It's the sound of a chainsaw.

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:27.590
It's the sound of trees falling.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.320
And by training a model
with those sounds,

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.066
by training a model
with those things

00:05:32.066 --> 00:05:33.440
and putting it
onto a cell phone,

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.060
having that cell
phone be connected

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.980
and then spreading them
through the forest--

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.810
instead of having
people going and trying

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.120
to monitor to see where
illegal logging is going on,

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.340
these things, once they
detect it, they dial home.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.844
And it's been cutting
illegal logging drastically.

00:05:46.844 --> 00:05:48.260
Another personal
favorite of mine,

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.940
and this one's a
little bit more fun,

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.935
is being able to use
the Google Arts app.

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:55.310
And this you can download and
play with on the Play Store

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.240
right now.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.040
All you've got to do
is you take a selfie.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.160
And then what it will do is
based on the pattern of what

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.010
it sees in that selfie,
it will match your face

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.600
with famous works of art.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.800
Like in this case, you can
see here this young lady.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.470
She has very distinctive eyes.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.270
And she's got a very
nice little smile.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.070
And her eyes and smile get
matched very, very nicely

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.276
to paintings like
this one and this one.

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.400
And if you try it yourself,
it can be a lot of fun.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.530
This morning I decided to sit
down and try it out for myself.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.130
And I got this.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.581
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:27.581 --> 00:06:30.080
Now, to me, the guy on the right
looks more like John Cleese

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:30.996
than he looks like me.

00:06:30.996 --> 00:06:32.870
But what can you do?

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.520
But then I thought, maybe I
can try to fool the algorithm.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.370
So I decided to put a cup of
coffee in front of my face.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.110
And I got this.

00:06:42.212 --> 00:06:44.420
So I guess it thought the
beard and the cup of coffee

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.710
looked very similar.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.460
You can have a whole
lot of fun with this.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.690
And again, it uses
machine learning.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.040
And the algorithm,
the model, has

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.010
been built using all
of this famous artwork.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.410
And then what it does is
that when I take a selfie

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:58.400
it matches my face.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.080
It extracts features
from my face,

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.420
and it tries to find artwork
with similar features.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.070
And then it matches
me accordingly.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.990
But what is machine learning?

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.660
You come to conferences
like this one.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.877
You come to maybe Google I/O.
You go to AI conferences.

00:07:11.877 --> 00:07:13.460
And you hear all
these great scenarios

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:16.880
about finding planets and curing
diseases and matching faces

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.010
with John Cleese or
John Cleese lookalikes.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:22.320
But what actually
is machine learning?

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.110
So the way I like
to look at it is

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.300
if you think about-- how many of
us here are coders, by the way?

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.550
How many programmers are here?

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.370
Oh, good.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:28.970
Most of us.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:29.600
OK.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:31.730
So classical programming,
when we think about it,

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:34.730
is you think about classical
programming as a black box.

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:37.340
And you put data and you put
rules into that black box.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.175
And you get answers out of it.

00:07:39.175 --> 00:07:40.550
So if I were to
think about-- say

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.270
if I'm building something
that's like a dogs versus cats

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.237
classifier.

00:07:45.237 --> 00:07:46.820
That would be really
difficult, right?

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.040
How do you tell the difference
between a dog and a cat?

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.480
You know, cats might
have pointy ears.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.070
And they're planning
to murder you.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:53.930
Dogs probably have
long floppy tongues.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.580
And they'll love you for
the rest of your life.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.200
But those things are
really hard to do in rules.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:57.740
Right?

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.376
Those things are
hard to do in coding.

00:07:59.376 --> 00:08:02.000
And most of us who are classical
programmers-- we've used Java,

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.980
we've used C# or C++ or
any of these things--

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:05.730
that's what we do.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:06.440
We write rules.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:07.520
If this, then that.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.260
While this, do that.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.730
All of those kinds of things.

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:12.440
And then those worlds
operate on data.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.287
We compile them into
something of value.

00:08:14.287 --> 00:08:16.370
And that something of value
gives us what we need.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.720
That gives us the answers out.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.000
Well, all machine learning
is, and the revolution

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.550
around machine learning, is it
flips the axis on this diagram.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.610
So machine learning is
you put the answers in,

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:28.400
you put the data in, and
you get the rules out.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.089
So it may not seem like much.

00:08:31.089 --> 00:08:33.380
But when you start thinking
about some of the scenarios

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.970
that now become possible--

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.549
I'm wearing a
smartwatch at the moment

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.230
which tracks my activity.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.726
This watch knows
when I'm walking.

00:08:40.726 --> 00:08:41.809
It knows when I'm running.

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.570
It knows when I'm swimming--
in my case probably trying

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:45.850
not to drown.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.210
And it can detect
all of these things.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.090
But think about
walking and running.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:52.659
Now, if you're a classical
programmer, how would

00:08:52.659 --> 00:08:54.200
you tell the difference
when somebody

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.330
is wearing a watch between
walking and running?

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:57.847
Maybe it's based on their speed.

00:08:57.847 --> 00:08:59.930
You could somehow be able
to infer the speed based

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.310
on the location of the watch.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.200
Maybe if the watch has
a heart rate sensor

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.760
it'd be based on
their heart rates.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.634
But some people when
they're running probably

00:09:06.634 --> 00:09:09.220
have a much lower heart rate
than I have when I'm walking.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.790
And how do you tell the
difference between when

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:11.630
I'm walking and running?

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.220
There are some times if
I'm going really fast I

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.600
might be walking downhill.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.730
And sometimes if I'm
going really slow

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.140
I might be running uphill.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.050
So all these if-thens-- it
becomes very, very difficult

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.240
to build something like
an activity tracker

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.950
like I have on this watch.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.600
But with machine
learning, you say, OK.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.240
I'm not going to
write if-then rules.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.990
I'm going to give it
all of the answers.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:34.550
When Apple created this watch--

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.700
and they've been doing it with
the recognition on this watch--

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.460
they've had thousands of people
exercising and saying, OK.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:41.404
Right now I'm running.

00:09:41.404 --> 00:09:42.320
Right now I'm walking.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:43.471
Right now I'm swimming.

00:09:43.471 --> 00:09:45.470
And from that they've
been able to build models.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:48.350
And anybody who builds any
kind of activity recognition

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:49.370
does the same thing.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:51.620
So now you're feeding into
this thing all the answers.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:52.910
This is what walking looks like.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.243
This is what running looks like.

00:09:54.243 --> 00:09:55.780
This is what
swimming looks like.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:57.180
And you feed it in the data.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.680
This is what it looks like.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.220
And as a result, when you
build a model around that,

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.419
then out of that
will come the rules.

00:10:03.419 --> 00:10:05.960
So now in future if you pass it
a bunch of data, it will say,

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:07.102
I think you're walking.

00:10:07.102 --> 00:10:08.060
I think you're running.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.380
I think you're swimming.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.716
And that's the whole
revolution in machine learning.

00:10:11.716 --> 00:10:13.340
It's not a lot when
you think about it.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.715
When you look at
the two diagrams

00:10:14.715 --> 00:10:16.096
they don't look very different.

00:10:16.096 --> 00:10:17.720
So what I always
encourage people to do

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:20.810
is think about the scenarios
that this empowers.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.710
Think about, for example,
the planet spotting

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:23.960
that I spoke about earlier on.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.180
That would not be easy
to do with if-then rules.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:27.320
It would be possible.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.550
But it's a lot of code
that you have to write.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.200
It's a lot of code that
you have to maintain.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.030
And then when you get new
data in that breaks the rules,

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:33.950
you probably have to
rewrite the rules.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.440
Think about things like
the diabetic retinopathy--

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.050
the same thing.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:40.550
So when we talk about TensorFlow
and when we're building out

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.230
TensorFlow, we always ask people
to just remember this diagram.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.540
This is all about you
getting your data,

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.200
understanding your data,
getting the answers--

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:49.809
that's called
labeling the data--

00:10:49.809 --> 00:10:51.350
and then having a
framework that will

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.532
allow you to munge them
together and build something

00:10:53.532 --> 00:10:54.740
that will give you inference.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.870
So that you give it
future data and then

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:59.164
it will tell you what the
class of that data is.

00:10:59.164 --> 00:11:00.830
So now there's a whole
bunch of products

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:02.020
that we've built at Google.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:02.990
You've probably
seen a bunch of them

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:04.156
this morning in the keynote.

00:11:04.156 --> 00:11:05.937
And I just wanted to
highlight one here.

00:11:05.937 --> 00:11:07.520
So the idea behind
these is that we've

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.170
been working on this
stuff for years.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:12.560
We've been working on how
to build something that

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.140
provides great inference, that
provides for great machine

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:15.710
learning.

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:17.630
And on top of that we've built
some products so that if there

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:20.660
are some scenarios that you want
to do-- like face detection,

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.690
logo identification, label
identification, et cetera--

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.940
then there's a product like
Cloud Vision that does it.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.440
But these aren't
different products.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.780
Cloud Vision is built
on top of TensorFlow.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:32.939
So TensorFlow is the
framework that I've mentioned.

00:11:32.939 --> 00:11:34.980
If you're not familiar
with it, it's open source.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:35.480
It's free.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:37.830
You can go download it
and you can play with it.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.740
And we define it as the machine
learning platform for everybody

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.330
to solve real problems.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.387
So I just wanted to set
the level of where we're

00:11:44.387 --> 00:11:46.470
at for this session before
I start going into some

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.428
of the new stuff and what
we've been working on

00:11:48.428 --> 00:11:49.480
in the last year.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.010
So here's where we've been
going in the last 12 months.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.990
So first of all, the number
one piece of feedback

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:56.910
that we've had from
software developers was,

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.500
would you please
support my language?

00:11:58.500 --> 00:11:59.520
I love TensorFlow.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.140
I love machine learning.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.760
I'm not a Pythonista.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:05.460
I really want to be able to
use things such as Swift.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.457
Or I really want to be able to
use things such as JavaScript.

00:12:08.457 --> 00:12:10.290
And as a result, these
are two of the things

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.456
that we've been working
hard to add to the platform.

00:12:12.456 --> 00:12:15.250
I'm going to be showing
some JavaScript stuff later.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:18.072
And I'm also doing a session
immediately after this one--

00:12:18.072 --> 00:12:20.280
and I can't remember if it's
upstairs or downstairs--

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.980
upstairs in the 3,000s.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:24.390
And I'll be showing some
JavaScript demos there.

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:27.630
So the idea is as a software
developer JavaScript

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:28.216
is a skill.

00:12:28.216 --> 00:12:29.590
It's a highly
sought after skill.

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.060
And it's a very common skill.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.000
And we really wanted to
bring these scenarios

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.500
of being able to
do machine learning

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:35.682
to JavaScript developers.

00:12:35.682 --> 00:12:37.140
Because then two
things can happen.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.780
Number one, you can actually
execute models directly

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:40.870
in the browser.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:42.990
And number two, you can
even train models directly

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:43.896
in the browser.

00:12:43.896 --> 00:12:45.270
There's a really
cool demo if you

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:49.110
go to js.tensorflow.org
where it's a Pac-Man game.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.110
And if you've ever seen the
Google Doodle of Pac-Man,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.050
if you remember that one, you
can actually train Pac-Man

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.095
based on the webcam.

00:12:56.095 --> 00:12:58.470
So you can point up, point
down, point left, point right.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.390
And then you can control
Pac-Man that way.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.410
It's all done
within the browser.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.350
So there's no
round-tripping of code

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.450
to Google or anywhere else.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:09.170
All of the training is done
in the browser of the model.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:11.966
You're passing it in the
answers by saying this is up.

00:13:11.966 --> 00:13:13.590
You're passing it in
the data by frames

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.962
of your finger pointing up.

00:13:14.962 --> 00:13:16.170
And then you train the model.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:17.503
And it only takes a few seconds.

00:13:17.503 --> 00:13:19.920
It's really cool stuff.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.530
But this then opens up
several different scenarios

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:23.700
for machine learning.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:25.074
And the direction
that we've been

00:13:25.074 --> 00:13:28.060
trying to take with TensorFlow
is really threefold.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.390
So number one, there's
TensorFlow itself.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.520
So there's the main
framework of TensorFlow.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.360
And this is designed to
allow you to train models

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:38.530
and to execute models
on a variety of devices.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.760
So what that provides you
is the APIs and the SDKs

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.410
and the ability
to take the data,

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.420
take the labels or the
answers into that data,

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.322
compile a model, and then
run inference on that model

00:13:48.322 --> 00:13:50.780
in a variety of places, serving
it off of a web browser, et

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.780
cetera, et cetera.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:55.980
But in addition to that this
year we've been focusing very

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.110
hard-- and it's part
of my core passion--

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:00.050
on something called
TensorFlow Lite.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:01.962
And the idea behind
TensorFlow Lite

00:14:01.962 --> 00:14:04.170
is that this is just for
executing models and running

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:05.120
inference.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.890
And it allows you to do it
on a variety of devices--

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.944
so Android, iOS.

00:14:09.944 --> 00:14:12.360
You've probably seen we've run
some demos on Raspberry Pi.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.040
And we're working
on more IoT devices.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.270
We'll have some announcements
about that shortly.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.810
So the idea here
is that sometimes

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:22.080
training a model you need a
big expensive workstation.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:25.590
You need maybe TPUs or
GPUs to train something.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:28.260
But you want to be able to
distribute that model to people

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:29.730
to be able to use it anywhere.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:32.580
Like the diabetic retinopathy
I spoke about earlier on--

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.600
wouldn't it be great if
places in the emerging

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:36.660
world with a cheap
cell phone could just

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:39.560
have a simple app with a
model running on that app?

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.060
And then they pass a photograph
of the cornea of an eye

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:42.840
into it.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:44.640
And then it will run
inference on that

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:47.820
and give you a prediction
about whether this person has

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.560
diabetic retinopathy or not.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.630
That's the idea behind
TensorFlow Lite--

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:54.060
to be able to take these
big models that you

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.190
build on workstations
but take them out

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.800
of the big enterprises, take
them out of the universities,

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.541
and give developers an
opportunity to build something

00:15:01.541 --> 00:15:03.540
that thousands of people
use, millions of people

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.710
can use, and to
change the world.

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:07.690
So that's our goal
behind TensorFlow Lite.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:08.930
Now, notice that's
just the run time.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.180
You're not going be doing
any training on the devices.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.990
Because they've got
relatively less power.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:15.720
And then thirdly
is TensorFlow.js.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:16.920
So this is JavaScript.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.830
And as I mentioned earlier
on, here's a case of now

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.022
if you want to distribute
training to people--

00:15:23.022 --> 00:15:24.480
the nice thing
about web technology

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:26.260
is with a web browser,
you type in a url

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.130
and your application executes
within that person's browser.

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:30.660
What if you have
a scenario where

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.470
people want to give you data?

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.520
Or you want to train a
model off of people's data

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.170
and off of the labels
that they give.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.420
You can now distribute
the gathering

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:40.830
of this data over the web.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.990
Either train it directly within
their browser or, of course,

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.156
have them send it back to
you where you can train it

00:15:45.156 --> 00:15:46.890
by working in JavaScript.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:48.360
And another nice
part about being

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.318
able to do this in
JavaScript is-- does anybody

00:15:50.318 --> 00:15:52.260
use Cloud Functions?

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:52.760
A few.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:55.200
Or Cloud Functions for Firebase?

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:55.800
OK.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.480
So as you know,
they run in Node.js.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:02.060
And if it runs in Node.js, now
by having JavaScript on there

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.620
you can actually
execute a function

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.140
to either run inference
on a machine-learned model

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:10.010
or to build a
machine-learned model.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:11.670
The nice thing about
Cloud Functions

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.121
is that they operate
off of triggers,

00:16:14.121 --> 00:16:16.370
where a trigger is something
happening in your network

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:17.977
or something happening
in your system.

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:20.060
For example, if a user
logs into your application,

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.772
that's a trigger.

00:16:22.772 --> 00:16:24.980
So instead of you explicitly
calling a piece of code,

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.770
you could say this has
triggered this code to execute.

00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:29.510
And you then may have
a scenario where,

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:31.430
OK, a user has logged in.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:34.910
I want to run an inference,
maybe to do something

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:38.090
like a capture, automatically
behind the scenes-- those kind

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:38.590
of things.

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:40.220
There's lots of scenarios.

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:44.050
It's up to you to think of
what would work best for you.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:46.870
So as I mentioned, one
of my passions is mobile.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.570
And so I'd like to talk a little
bit about TensorFlow Lite.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.070
So this is what
it's like to use it.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.360
So this is Java code
running on Android.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:58.159
So if you're a
Kotlinista as well,

00:16:58.159 --> 00:17:00.450
if you like Kotlin, of course
this will work in Kotlin.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:03.970
If you're an iOS developer,
right now it only works in C++.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:07.060
We haven't built the projections
into Objective-C or Swift

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:08.200
yet for it.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.270
So you'd have to write
C++ to do the inference.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:11.650
But we're working on that.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.599
But all you have to
do is very simply

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:17.440
there is a package called
org.tensorflow.lite.interpreter.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.109
And on this one you
would load your model.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:21.977
So earlier I spoke
about building a model.

00:17:21.977 --> 00:17:22.810
You give it answers.

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:23.518
You give it data.

00:17:23.518 --> 00:17:24.579
It gives you rules.

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.990
You compile that model
using TensorFlow.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:30.010
That model from TensorFlow then,
to be able to run on mobile,

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.870
uses a process
called flattening.

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:33.752
The idea is you take
that and it flattens it.

00:17:33.752 --> 00:17:34.960
And it makes it more compact.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:37.089
And it removes some of
the larger floating point

00:17:37.089 --> 00:17:38.380
operations and stuff like that.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:42.250
It makes it smaller so it
will run on a mobile device.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:45.220
And the file extension
for that is .tflite.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:47.910
Which is why you see here
new Interpreter("awesome

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:48.820
model.tflite").

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:49.930
And then all you
have to do with that

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:51.070
is you pass it your inputs.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.880
And it gives you
back the outputs.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:54.130
So for example-- I don't know.

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.740
If you're writing a
classifier like I mentioned

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.080
for a diabetic
retinopathy, your input

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:01.600
would be your
image or how you've

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.057
processed that image so that
the model would recognize it.

00:18:04.057 --> 00:18:05.890
And your outputs that
it would give you back

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:10.220
would be a probability of this
person having that disease.

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:10.720
[COUGHS]

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:12.682
Excuse me.

00:18:12.682 --> 00:18:15.140
The performance of TensorFlow
Lite is actually really nice.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.449
As you can see here,
we did this benchmark.

00:18:17.449 --> 00:18:19.490
And there's famous machine
learning models called

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:20.930
MobileNet and
Inception which are

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:22.740
used for image classification.

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.190
And when we flattened
the model, when

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.470
we ran it on TensorFlow
Lite on an Android device

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:30.560
or on an iOS device and we
did some benchmarking in Q1

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:33.830
of this year, these are
the performance increases

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:35.180
that we've actually seen.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.580
So the orange bar is
running it on TensorFlow

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.290
on a traditional workstation.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:42.410
The gray bar is running it on
TensorFlow Lite on a mobile.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:44.600
And we can see that
inference is very, very fast.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:47.150
And I'm going to show a
demo in a moment of me doing

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.740
a live image classification.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:52.149
And you'll see how fast that
it will actually execute.

00:18:52.149 --> 00:18:53.440
Actually, I'll do it right now.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.870
So if we can switch
to the demo machine.

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:58.291
So I need to sign in.

00:18:58.291 --> 00:18:58.790
Sorry.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:08.590
OK.

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:12.440
So I am actually going to run
this in the Android emulator,

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:15.740
which I know is incredibly
brave of me, live on stage.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:17.870
So as you'll see it's running.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:20.040
So I'm running the
Android emulator here.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.750
And you can see the webcam on
the Android Emulator is going.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.120
And it's actually
classifying three things.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.670
So what's happening is this is--

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:31.630
MobileNet has been trained with
a thousand different images.

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.106
So there's a neural
network that's

00:19:33.106 --> 00:19:34.480
been trained to
detect a thousand

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.167
different types of image.

00:19:36.167 --> 00:19:37.750
So what happens is
now every time it's

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:39.400
capturing a frame
from the webcam,

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:43.264
or from the front cam on
the emulated phone, what

00:19:43.264 --> 00:19:44.930
it's actually doing
is it's passing that

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:46.390
to this compiled model.

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:48.100
This compiled model
is coming back then

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:51.700
with a set of probabilities
of each of these 1,000.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:53.810
So like right now, I'm
not showing it a banana.

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:58.270
So it's setting at a
probability of less than .011%

00:19:58.270 --> 00:19:59.440
that it's a banana.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:01.690
And then I'm taking
the top three

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:04.450
that it actually gave me back
and then representing them.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:07.440
So we have this grid
on the wall behind us.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.360
Obviously it hasn't
been trained for that.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:11.380
But that kind of
looks like a strainer.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:14.380
And as a result, it's saying,
hey, that's a strainer.

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:17.380
As you can see then
above that, that 1,385

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.150
milliseconds-- that's
the amount of time

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:20.686
it's taken to do the inference.

00:20:20.686 --> 00:20:22.060
But consider that
that's actually

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:26.245
running on the emulator on
my desktop as I'm speaking.

00:20:26.245 --> 00:20:27.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:27.880
What?

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:29.004
Did it say something funny?

00:20:29.004 --> 00:20:30.850
Sometimes it says
dachshund or something

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:31.974
when I step in front of it.

00:20:31.974 --> 00:20:33.010
Let's see.

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:34.140
I'm an Italian Greyhound.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:34.680
Yay!

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.940
I wish I was that fast.

00:20:39.940 --> 00:20:41.375
But like if I--

00:20:41.375 --> 00:20:43.750
if you're familiar with "Silicon
Valley" and the hot dog,

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:44.920
not hot dog thing.

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:46.753
I don't know how it
will work in this light.

00:20:46.753 --> 00:20:47.824
So we'll see.

00:20:47.824 --> 00:20:49.240
But this is my
famous banana demo.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:52.360
But let's see if it
says it's a banana.

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:53.020
There we go!

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:57.670
100% probability
that it was a banana.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:58.750
I had to put this behind.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:20:59.374
I was cheating.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:06.370
The MobileNet model is a famous
model amongst researchers.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:08.080
They've taken a thousand images.

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:10.420
We've built this neural
network that can then classify

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:11.890
amongst those thousand images.

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:13.780
Strainer, shower
curtain, and all those

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:15.040
are three of those images.

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:17.470
Banana and Italian
greyhound are others.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.596
And this is now running on
Android as you can see here.

00:21:20.596 --> 00:21:21.720
And it's doing pretty well.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.040
It's only taking like
1,100 milliseconds

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:27.100
to actually identify something
when running on an emulator.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:28.640
I also have this running on my--

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:29.721
I have a Pixel 2 phone.

00:21:29.721 --> 00:21:30.970
I have it running on my phone.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:33.511
If you see me later and you want
to see a demo of how fast it

00:21:33.511 --> 00:21:36.070
is on actual hardware-- then it
can do this inference usually

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:38.590
in 50 milliseconds or less.

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:41.260
So if we can switch back
to the slides, please.

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:44.850
I'm a keyboard.

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:45.510
I'm a sock.

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:46.560
I'm a strainer.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.300
I'm a running shoe.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:49.800
Yeah.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:51.480
Can we switch back to the
slides please, before I

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:52.470
embarrass myself further?

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:53.060
I'm a hamster!

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:58.709
So the next one
that I mentioned--

00:21:58.709 --> 00:22:00.250
remember, there were
three run times.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:00.740
There's TensorFlow.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:02.000
There's TensorFlow Lite.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.500
And then there's TensorFlow.js.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:05.600
So with TensorFlow.js,
the idea is

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.320
that we've been working hard.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.130
We released this in
March of this year.

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.100
And we see the two
main scenarios for this

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:15.590
as, number one, to be able
to do training or inference

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:16.760
within the browser.

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.516
It takes advantage of
things such as WebGL

00:22:18.516 --> 00:22:19.890
when it's running
in the browser.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:21.452
So it can be very, very fast.

00:22:21.452 --> 00:22:23.660
And then on the other side
of it, if you have servers

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.300
and you want to execute
stuff on a Node.js server,

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:28.550
be that something like
a Cloud Function or just

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.790
your own Node.js server,
then you can do so as well.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:33.290
And the really neat thing, like
I've mentioned, about this--

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:35.930
and I've know I'm repeating
myself, but it is really cool--

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:39.600
is the fact that you can do
both training and inference.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.960
So you can actually present
somebody with a web page.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:42.770
They give you data.

00:22:42.770 --> 00:22:45.150
And it will train a model
based on their data.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.240
And then they can execute
inference on that.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:49.700
Or you can build a model,
such as the MobileNet

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:52.070
model that I've mentioned,
which is the one that I just

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:53.630
used for the image
classification,

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:55.850
download to that
browser, and then retrain

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.210
the final layer of that with
classifications of their own.

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:00.920
And that's how the
Pac-Man demo works.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:02.990
If you go to
js.tensorflow.org and take

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:04.539
a look at the Pac-Man
demo, that will

00:23:04.539 --> 00:23:06.830
be trained with four new
classes of image-- one for up,

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:08.570
one for down, one for
left, one for right.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:09.410
You can do it however you like.

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.332
I tend to use my
finger for training it.

00:23:11.332 --> 00:23:13.790
Or some people will actually
look up, look down, look left,

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:14.760
look right.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.240
And while it's a fun little
demo, think about the impact

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.760
that that could have on
things such as accessibility.

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.340
You know, sometimes we build
accessibility into products.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:26.389
But the accessibility tends to
be a common solution for the--

00:23:26.389 --> 00:23:28.680
I don't want to use the term
lowest common denominator,

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:29.840
but you know what I mean--

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:32.180
for the mass denominator
in this case.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.340
But it might be that
for an individual

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:36.380
the accessibility
might work differently.

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.466
Maybe they want to scroll
by going like this.

00:23:38.466 --> 00:23:40.340
Maybe they want to scroll
by going like this.

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:40.881
I don't know.

00:23:40.881 --> 00:23:42.380
Or scroll by nodding their head.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:45.650
But you could actually
use an image classifier

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:48.320
in the browser,
retrained in the browser,

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:51.680
for different, say, scroll or
click or something like that.

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:53.660
So that accessibility
could be customized

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:54.980
to a particular user.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:57.670
These are the kind of scenarios
that are now becoming possible.

00:23:57.670 --> 00:23:58.670
We can't build them all.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.300
We can't even think of them all.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.960
And that's why we like to talk
about TensorFlow with folks

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:03.907
like you.

00:24:03.907 --> 00:24:05.990
I know there are people
in this audience right now

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:07.709
who've got fabulous scenarios.

00:24:07.709 --> 00:24:10.250
And they just haven't had the
tools with which to build them.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:12.375
And we're hoping that the
tools that we're building

00:24:12.375 --> 00:24:15.130
will unblock you from
being able to do that.

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:16.450
So coding.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.540
So perhaps the biggest
changes in TensorFlow,

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:22.450
as well as adding this new
stuff, is in some of the coding

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:25.000
that we've been able to provide.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:28.180
So if you've been a TensorFlow
developer up to now,

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:32.786
and you are a developer
rather than an academic,

00:24:32.786 --> 00:24:34.660
if you've come from a
development background,

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.730
then the coding of
TensorFlow models

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:39.160
might have seemed a
little alien to you.

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:40.936
The idea was you define a model.

00:24:40.936 --> 00:24:43.060
You end up doing something
called building a graph.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:44.518
You build everything
ahead of time.

00:24:44.518 --> 00:24:46.960
And then you execute the
graph to do the training.

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:49.000
As software developers--
I don't know about you.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:51.220
I can't write more than
three lines of code

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.517
without having a bug in it.

00:24:52.517 --> 00:24:54.850
And as a result, I like to
write code two or three lines

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:57.010
at a time, run them, test them,
write the next two or three

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:58.362
lines, run them, test them.

00:24:58.362 --> 00:24:59.820
And that's traditional
programming.

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:02.470
And that didn't really work
very well with TensorFlow.

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:05.440
So we have something that
we put into TensorFlow

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:07.240
which I'm going to show.

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.970
Actually, I went to
the last one first.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:12.910
We put something into TensorFlow
that we called eager execution.

00:25:12.910 --> 00:25:14.560
And the idea behind
eager execution

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:16.690
is what that gives
you is the ability

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:19.030
to be like a pure
Python programmer

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.500
but continue to build
TensorFlow models.

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:23.770
You get things such as
step-by-step debugging.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:27.250
You get the immediacy of
being a real Python developer.

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:29.650
And then also as you're
building your models, if you're

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:32.052
familiar with things
such as gradient descent,

00:25:32.052 --> 00:25:33.760
and there's differentiation
and math that

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:35.400
needs to be done
to determine loss

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:37.780
and all that kind of
thing-- you don't lose that.

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:40.170
There's something called
a gradient tape in there.

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:43.030
And based on the named tape,
if you're from the VHS world

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.680
like I used to be, the idea
is that's recording everything

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:46.180
on tape.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.220
And that tape you can go back
and forward and all that kind

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:48.872
of stuff.

00:25:48.872 --> 00:25:50.830
So it's like you're not
losing any of the value

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:54.535
that you had when you were
building graph-based stuff

00:25:54.535 --> 00:25:57.160
by changing to immediacy because
things such as a gradient tape

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.030
are there.

00:25:58.030 --> 00:26:00.150
But let me skip back.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.449
So the other big
change, and to me

00:26:02.449 --> 00:26:03.990
the other really
exciting big change,

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:05.880
is in how you build models.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.250
So we've been working
really closely with Keras.

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:10.910
And Francois Chollet,
who invented Keras,

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:11.930
now works for Google.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:13.630
And he works on our team.

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:15.520
And so he has been
bringing Keras

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:18.570
to TensorFlow and the
tf.keras namespace.

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:20.039
And if you've done
any construction

00:26:20.039 --> 00:26:21.830
of neural networks, if
you've done anything

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.705
like that, if you want
to get into the really

00:26:23.705 --> 00:26:25.860
complicated stuff,
the idea behind Keras

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.660
is that it makes it really easy.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.937
Now, some of the terms
that you see on the slide

00:26:29.937 --> 00:26:31.770
here, if you're not a
neural network person,

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:33.390
may seem unintuitive to you.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:34.830
And I'm really sorry about that.

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.330
But the idea is when
you think about it, when

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.030
you define a neural
network, generally

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:40.080
you're defining
layers of neurons.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:41.490
And then in each
layer of neurons

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:43.074
you're defining behaviors.

00:26:43.074 --> 00:26:45.240
And in those behaviors,
there's generally two things

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:46.530
that you define.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:48.584
One is called an
activation function.

00:26:48.584 --> 00:26:51.000
And this is what it takes to
actually activate that neuron

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.250
and how it behaves
when it is activated.

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:55.180
And the other one is
called a loss function.

00:26:55.180 --> 00:26:57.330
And this is what
type of function

00:26:57.330 --> 00:26:59.716
am I going to use to
minimize loss in this layer.

00:26:59.716 --> 00:27:01.590
If you're not a neural
network person, sorry.

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:03.590
A lot of that would be
kind of gibberish to you.

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.400
But the code to do
this was actually

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:07.982
very complicated before Keras.

00:27:07.982 --> 00:27:09.690
And with Keras here,
here's how I can now

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:11.160
define a neural network.

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.050
I'm defining it and
it's just like, hey, I'm

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:14.414
creating a sequential network.

00:27:14.414 --> 00:27:16.080
So I have a number
of layers and they're

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:17.490
operating in a sequence.

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:20.910
Here's my shape of
my input, 28 by 28.

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:22.470
My first layer of
my network-- well,

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:23.720
this is what it's going to be.

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.690
It's a convolutional,
two-dimensional one.

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:28.200
And my activation in this
is something called a ReLu.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.934
And I'll continue to define
my network like that.

00:27:30.934 --> 00:27:33.350
This kind of code to be able
to define a network like this

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.766
might have taken dozens, if
not hundreds, of lines of code

00:27:35.766 --> 00:27:36.420
previously.

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:39.930
But with Keras, as you can
see, if you're a neural network

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.730
person, it's intuitive to you.

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:44.580
If you are just learning
this for the first time,

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:47.900
a lot of these terms will
seem very difficult to you.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:49.470
But actually as
you start learning

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.053
and as you start
growing in it, you'll

00:27:51.053 --> 00:27:53.460
see that this has actually
really simplified it for you.

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:56.150
Think about it in terms of
object-oriented development.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:57.630
You're creating an
object, which is

00:27:57.630 --> 00:27:59.250
your sequential neural network.

00:27:59.250 --> 00:28:00.960
And you're setting
properties on that.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:02.810
You're saying this
layer looks like this

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:03.500
and has this function.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.541
This layer looks like this
and has this function.

00:28:05.541 --> 00:28:07.930
And it's really
as simple as that.

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:09.180
Then there's input processing.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:10.680
The most important
thing when you're

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:12.180
defining a neural
network is you've

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:13.540
got to feed it good data.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:15.240
If you remember
my diagram earlier

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:17.573
on where I said you give it
data and you give it answers

00:28:17.573 --> 00:28:20.160
and it infers the rules, you've
got to give it good data.

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:21.390
Garbage in, garbage out.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:25.470
Or if you remember
Johnny 5, "need input."

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.240
So being able to
give it good data--

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:30.090
you need to be able to have
the tools to define your data,

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:33.720
to take dirty data that you
might have and clean it,

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:35.520
or to have tools to
take data that you have

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:38.640
and do things like normalize it
or make it easier to understand

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.020
for neural network.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:41.770
And then also, more
importantly, if you're

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:44.490
building giant neural networks
that take a lot of training,

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.110
as you're feeding data in
you want to be efficient.

00:28:47.110 --> 00:28:48.720
If you feed in a
terabyte of data

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:50.901
and then wait 100
hours for it to train,

00:28:50.901 --> 00:28:52.650
and then you have
another terabyte of data

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:54.275
and you wait 100
hours for it to train,

00:28:54.275 --> 00:28:55.360
that's not very efficient.

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:57.330
So there's a concept
called data pipelines

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:59.710
where you can optimize
your input of data

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:01.800
to the time it takes
to train the data.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.675
And we've been building
that out in something

00:29:03.675 --> 00:29:05.192
called the tf.data namespace.

00:29:05.192 --> 00:29:06.900
So instead of you just
taking random data

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:11.400
in a data structure like NumPy
or lists in Swift or something

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:13.620
along those lines, if you
actually take your data

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:15.850
and you format it with
the tf.data namespace,

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:17.550
it's synchronized
with the training.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.190
As a result, you can make
your input data and training

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:22.610
pipeline much more efficient.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:24.890
So eager I've
mentioned earlier on.

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.900
So now the next
thing is we've been

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:30.600
working really hard on these
things called Cloud TPUs.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.360
So T in TPU stands for Tensor.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:36.261
So the name TensorFlow comes
from the flow of tensors

00:29:36.261 --> 00:29:37.010
through a network.

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:39.530
And tensors are a way that
you can define your data

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:41.970
so that they're optimized
for neural networks

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:44.070
and for neural networks
to process them.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:46.700
So we said we wanted
to build a chip that's

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:49.582
designed specifically
for processing tensors.

00:29:49.582 --> 00:29:51.290
And that's why we came
up with this thing

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:54.200
that we call the TPU, the
Tensor Processing Unit.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:58.430
They are highly, highly, highly
optimized for TensorFlow.

00:29:58.430 --> 00:30:00.980
And as a result, you can see
some of the data about this

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:01.926
here.

00:30:01.926 --> 00:30:03.050
It's high bandwidth memory.

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:05.000
There's 180 teraflops
per device, which

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.482
apparently is quite a lot.

00:30:06.482 --> 00:30:07.940
But I'd rather look
at the results.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:08.440
Oh, sorry.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:12.480
Before I go to them, I'd like
to use an analogy for that.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.690
So think about when you're
training a neural network

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:17.300
on your laptop or
on your desktop PC.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:19.250
That probably has a CPU.

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:21.050
And a CPU, the
idea behind that is

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:24.560
that's a chip that's designed
for processing anything.

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:27.500
It's designed for managing
the windows on your screen.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:29.420
It's designed for
your networking stack.

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.003
It's designed for
all of these things.

00:30:31.003 --> 00:30:33.030
And it's a little bit
like a Swiss army knife.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:34.880
It's designed to be
able to do everything.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:37.550
But it's not particularly
optimized for anything.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:40.010
So as a result, it's not
optimized for training

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:41.210
and machine learning.

00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:42.950
And maybe you want
to build a model.

00:30:42.950 --> 00:30:45.350
And that model might take you
a month to build on a CPU.

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.350
Or it might take a year,
depending on the amount

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:49.140
of data that you have.

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:52.820
So the idea with a TPU is that
it's a highly optimized, highly

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:53.930
focused knife.

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:55.970
It's like a sushi chef's knife.

00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:57.784
So if you've ever
known a sushi chef,

00:30:57.784 --> 00:31:00.200
or if you've ever sat at a bar
where the people are making

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:03.320
sushi, they have these highly
optimized, highly sharp knives

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.124
that they can use to cut the
fish without taking bones

00:31:06.124 --> 00:31:07.790
and all of those kind
of things with it.

00:31:07.790 --> 00:31:08.750
And I always like to say--

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.490
people go, why would
I want to use a TPU?

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:12.004
Why would I want
to pay for a TPU?

00:31:12.004 --> 00:31:13.420
And think about
it in those terms.

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:15.794
If you need to train a model
and you've got a lot of data

00:31:15.794 --> 00:31:17.690
to train that model
on, like the thousands

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:20.480
or hundreds of thousands of
hours it takes to do something

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:22.102
like activity
recognition on a watch,

00:31:22.102 --> 00:31:23.810
you want that training
to happen quickly.

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:26.060
And you want it to be
optimized for actually

00:31:26.060 --> 00:31:27.104
training that model.

00:31:27.104 --> 00:31:28.520
And so I always
like to say, well,

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:30.047
if you're going to
use a CPU, you're

00:31:30.047 --> 00:31:32.630
using a Swiss army knife to make
sushi, which you don't really

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:34.370
want to do.

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:36.830
So taking one common
model called ResNet

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:39.380
for image classification,
here's some of the results

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:42.230
that we got from
using a TPU on it.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.690
So the number of
images per second

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:48.810
being fed in using
tf.data were about 2,700.

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:51.120
And synthetic
images-- so the idea

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:52.550
was you take in
the real image, we

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:55.100
do some processing on that
to make a synthetic image,

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:56.433
and then they were being fed in.

00:31:56.433 --> 00:31:58.070
It was over 3,000 per second.

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:02.639
And we found that we were able
to train it to 76% accuracy--

00:32:02.639 --> 00:32:04.430
so that is, when you
do the classification,

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:07.610
like I was doing earlier on,
it's right 76% of the time--

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:08.240
on ImageNet.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:13.430
But doing this on a TPU,
on a Tensor Processing

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:17.342
Unit, the cost for doing
this was actually about $85

00:32:17.342 --> 00:32:18.050
when we did this.

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:20.910
Now compare that to if
you were to buy a GPU,

00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:23.840
or many universities who
are renting access to GPUs.

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:26.494
The cost used to be in the
tens of thousands of dollars.

00:32:26.494 --> 00:32:28.660
They've brought it down to
the thousands of dollars.

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:31.310
There was one experiment-- we
designed this slide actually

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:34.520
for a presentation that Jeff
Dean was giving back in April.

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:37.160
And we put it with $85 on there.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.360
Before he went on stage,
somebody did another test.

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:41.660
And they actually had it
significantly lower than that.

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:43.130
I'm not allowed
to say the number.

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:46.070
But the point is it's
actually going down sharply.

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:50.060
So think about building a
really, really complex model.

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:52.846
You're talking about now it
can cost in the tens of dollars

00:32:52.846 --> 00:32:54.470
instead of in the
thousands or hundreds

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:55.572
of thousands of dollars.

00:32:55.572 --> 00:32:57.030
And if you take a
look at this link

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:58.904
you can actually see
the benchmark submission

00:32:58.904 --> 00:33:00.794
that we put in at Stanford.

00:33:00.794 --> 00:33:03.460
So some of the other things that
we've been trying to do is that

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:08.080
as TensorFlow is moving
towards version 1.10 very soon,

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:11.090
it's now time to bring more
developers into the fold.

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:13.990
It's becoming less of an
academic infrastructure

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:14.949
and academic framework.

00:33:14.949 --> 00:33:17.448
And we're trying to make it
more and more developer friendly

00:33:17.448 --> 00:33:19.930
so developers can start
building real things with it--

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:22.320
things such as the cell
phone that I spoke about

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.050
and scenarios with that one.

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:26.830
And as a result, we launched
a blog on blog.tensorflow.org.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:28.540
And we launched a
YouTube channel.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:30.230
I personally run
the YouTube channel.

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:32.757
So if you've got any ideas
for videos or any questions

00:33:32.757 --> 00:33:34.090
you'll see me all over that one.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:35.450
And please reach out.

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:39.160
I'm happy to create any videos
for scenarios that you want

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:40.746
to see and want to learn about.

00:33:40.746 --> 00:33:43.120
Also what we've been trying
to do is to empower everybody

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:45.852
to learn and explore
machine learning

00:33:45.852 --> 00:33:48.310
through something called the
Machine Learning Crash Course.

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:49.934
And it's this link
at the bottom here--

00:33:49.934 --> 00:33:52.280
g.co/machinelearningcrashcourse.

00:33:52.280 --> 00:33:54.060
It's a place where
you can learn it.

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:56.890
And I'm not trying to
be salesman-y about it

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:58.810
or anything, but
this was the course

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:01.270
that we built to train us.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:02.710
So I learned machine learning--

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:04.630
I used to work on
Firebase at Google.

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:06.580
I learned machine
learning while I

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:09.070
was on the Firebase team
using the internal version

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:09.710
of this course.

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:11.918
So this is what we've used
to train the actual Google

00:34:11.918 --> 00:34:14.519
engineers who are
building the thing.

00:34:14.519 --> 00:34:16.560
So some other things that
we've been working on--

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:18.730
TensorFlow Hub is the next one.

00:34:18.730 --> 00:34:21.030
So if you think
about models, there

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:24.000
are common scenarios for
models like image recognition

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:26.761
or translation or
text classification.

00:34:26.761 --> 00:34:29.219
So we said, we want to be able
to create a hub where people

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:32.070
can download those models and
use them, and maybe even use

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:34.002
parts of them in
their own models.

00:34:34.002 --> 00:34:35.460
If you're familiar
with TensorFlow,

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:38.051
a lot of the models that we've
done we've just put on GitHub.

00:34:38.051 --> 00:34:39.300
And you know, GitHub is great.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:42.250
You can download a model off
of that and you can use it.

00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:44.400
But it's not optimized
for something like this.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:45.540
It's not a catalog.

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:47.100
It's not searchable.

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:50.070
It's not easy for everybody
to share in one place.

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:52.320
So the idea is that we wanted
to build TensorFlow Hub,

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:55.650
or TF-Hub as we call it,
so that you can find models

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:56.520
that you can reuse.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:58.440
You can find models
that you can build on,

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:00.520
or of course, you can
share your own models.

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:04.300
And again it's at that
url, tensorflow.org/hub.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:08.090
So one of the things that's
kind of coming in the future--

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:09.605
we're still working hard on it--

00:35:09.605 --> 00:35:11.260
that we're really
passionate about now

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:14.340
is what we call learn
to learn, or AutoML.

00:35:14.340 --> 00:35:15.590
And the idea is we said, OK.

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:18.830
Well, what if today you
want to build a solution?

00:35:18.830 --> 00:35:20.001
What do you need?

00:35:20.001 --> 00:35:21.500
You need machine
learning expertise.

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:23.780
You need people who
understand Python or whatever

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:25.370
the language--

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:27.820
whatever languages I was
talking about earlier-- can do.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:29.820
You need them to be able
to understand the data,

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.329
how to format the data and
put that into pipelines,

00:35:32.329 --> 00:35:33.620
how to design a neural network.

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:34.947
It's a lot of expertise.

00:35:34.947 --> 00:35:36.530
Of course you would
need all the data.

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.460
And you also need the
computation power.

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:40.376
This is what the world
looks like today if you

00:35:40.376 --> 00:35:41.780
want to build a good solution.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:45.410
We said, what if we could
take the deep ML expertise out

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:49.430
of that and just say
data plus lots and lots

00:35:49.430 --> 00:35:50.510
and lots of computation?

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:52.010
Because machine
learning, after all,

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:55.370
is you feed it in the answers,
you feed it in the data,

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:58.250
and it comes up with
an inference from it.

00:35:58.250 --> 00:36:00.750
So if you remember this slide
that I showed you earlier on--

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:02.970
I've been working on TensorFlow
for about six months.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:05.420
I still don't understand half
of the words on this slide.

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:07.610
Not many people do.

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.290
I don't know why,
for example, I would

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:13.010
use a MaxPooling of (2,2),
(2,2) instead of (3,3), (3,3),

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:15.140
other than a lot
of experimentation,

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:16.844
a lot of trial,
and a lot of error.

00:36:16.844 --> 00:36:19.010
There are people who can
understand a neural network

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.110
and understand the math
behind a neural network

00:36:21.110 --> 00:36:23.630
and who may be able to write
better code than I would here.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:25.250
But a lot of the time when
I'm building something

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:27.130
like this it's a lot
of trial and error.

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:28.850
It's a lot of, OK, where
am I going to flatten it?

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:30.183
Where am I going to do dropouts?

00:36:30.183 --> 00:36:32.480
Where am I going to do
all these kind of things?

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:35.510
And there's a bit of a black
box element in designing

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:38.480
these neural networks.

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:40.490
There are academic
papers coming out

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:42.380
on a daily basis that
say, well, if you want

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:44.860
to do image recognition, you
should do this many dropouts

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:46.360
or you should do
that many dropouts.

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:49.410
Or you should use a ReLu or
you should use something else.

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:53.360
It's not as prescriptive as--
well, in many ways it could be.

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:55.730
I was about to say it's not
as prescriptive as writing

00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:56.445
typical code.

00:36:56.445 --> 00:36:58.820
But then sometimes-- well, is
it better to do a for loop?

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:00.352
Is it better to do a while loop?

00:37:00.352 --> 00:37:02.060
These kind of things
are typical of code.

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:04.187
And it's the same
kind of thing that

00:37:04.187 --> 00:37:06.270
will happen when you're
designing neural networks.

00:37:06.270 --> 00:37:08.394
Should I design it with
this number of input layers

00:37:08.394 --> 00:37:10.309
or this type of function?

00:37:10.309 --> 00:37:12.350
So we said, well, what if
with this kind of code,

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:15.110
instead of a human doing a lot
of trial and error behind that,

00:37:15.110 --> 00:37:17.030
we actually have a
computer do the trial

00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:18.320
and error behind that?

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:19.020
Then we say, OK.

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:21.740
We know roughly what our neural
network should look like.

00:37:21.740 --> 00:37:24.077
And we know roughly what
kind of activation functions

00:37:24.077 --> 00:37:25.910
should be on there and
what kind of dropouts

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:27.210
and all that kind of stuff.

00:37:27.210 --> 00:37:30.740
And what if we then, say, take
that and then train on that,

00:37:30.740 --> 00:37:33.200
but then we try lots
of different ones?

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:34.460
We change the function here.

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:36.549
We change the design
of a layer there,

00:37:36.549 --> 00:37:37.590
all these kind of things.

00:37:37.590 --> 00:37:39.570
And we iterate and
iterate and iterate.

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.240
And then we evaluate
those models.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:44.307
Then we could actually have a
computer learning how to learn.

00:37:44.307 --> 00:37:46.640
We could actually build a
machine learning kind of thing

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:47.810
that learns how to learn.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:49.550
Now, the computational
expense for this

00:37:49.550 --> 00:37:51.187
is really, really
big right now, which

00:37:51.187 --> 00:37:53.270
is probably why Google are
the only ones doing it.

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:54.894
But with Moore's Law
being Moore's Law,

00:37:54.894 --> 00:37:56.330
it's getting cheaper over time.

00:37:56.330 --> 00:37:59.610
And as a result, we can
actually start thinking about,

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:02.300
in the future, building
neural architectures that

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:05.230
optimize themselves.

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:07.650
So some of the results that
we've had out of this is that

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.720
when we take a look at some of
the hand-crafted models that

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:11.640
are out there, that
have been built by some

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:13.800
of the world's
greatest researchers--

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.750
if you take a look
at the x-axis here,

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.690
the x-axis here is the number
of operations of computation

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:19.470
that are needed.

00:38:19.470 --> 00:38:21.467
Just see that as the
expense, ultimately,

00:38:21.467 --> 00:38:23.550
even though that expense
is coming down over time.

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.670
And the y-axis is the accuracy
and precision of these image

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:27.786
recognition models.

00:38:27.786 --> 00:38:29.160
So if we take a
look at something

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:31.930
like Inception, which you
can see in the lower left,

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:34.320
that's a very cheap
and very easy model.

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:37.110
And the accuracy of
it is around 70%.

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:38.670
Or MobileNet, which
is the one that I

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:40.617
was using when I was
classifying on my phone,

00:38:40.617 --> 00:38:42.200
you can see it's a
pretty cheap model.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:43.734
And the accuracy is about 70%.

00:38:43.734 --> 00:38:45.900
But when we start looking
at all the research that's

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:49.320
been done on this, and we go
all the way over to the right,

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:52.800
you can see that the cost of
that is orders of magnitude

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:53.700
higher.

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:56.460
But the actual accuracy isn't.

00:38:56.460 --> 00:39:00.130
It's going to 80% or
82% instead of 70%.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:00.790
So we said, OK.

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:04.890
What if we can compare a
network that learns itself

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:07.770
rather than something that was
designed by a human to this?

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:09.960
And this is what we got.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:12.090
So when we started
training on small models,

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:14.580
as you can see on
the left, we actually

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.460
knocked things like MobileNet
and Inception out of the water.

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:20.520
So for the same cost we had
far more accurate models.

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:22.530
And then when we go
over onto the right

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.440
we're in the same ballpark,
if not slightly better.

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:29.040
So for really, really large
data sets and really, really--

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:31.470
so instead of 1,000 images,
maybe 100,000 images

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.030
it's able to
classify-- we've been

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:35.340
able to find that we're
in the same ballpark

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:36.780
by having these networks.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:38.910
Instead of a human
designing this network

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:41.130
and testing this network
and figuring it out,

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:43.840
the human gives us the base
that we're going to start from.

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.470
But then it learns and it
improves itself over time.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:49.260
And if you remember
the AlphaGo that

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:51.420
beat Lee Sedol, the
champion, but then there

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:53.010
was another version
of AlphaGo which

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:56.400
learned by itself that beat
the one that beat Lee Sedol--

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:00.500
it's the same kind of technology
that was in use there.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:03.230
So just going back to some
of the impact you can have--

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:04.813
and we've been working
on it and learn

00:40:04.813 --> 00:40:07.770
to learn for the diabetic
retinopathy as well.

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:10.430
Again, comparing with humans--

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:12.680
taking humans and
the data that humans

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:15.650
have been able to give us to
train a model but then using

00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:17.459
learn to learn to
improve on that model.

00:40:17.459 --> 00:40:19.250
That's where we've been
getting the results

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:21.260
in diabetic retinopathy
that are actually

00:40:21.260 --> 00:40:24.752
improving over what the top
ophthalmologists can do.

00:40:24.752 --> 00:40:26.210
And again, I just
want to reiterate

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:28.550
this is not a replacement
for ophthalmologists.

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:31.550
The goal here is that if an
ophthalmologist can manually

00:40:31.550 --> 00:40:33.290
do this and see
five people a day,

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:34.665
but if they have
a tool like this

00:40:34.665 --> 00:40:38.560
that allows them to see 100
people a day, everybody wins.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:41.710
So with that in mind, just
remember the whole goal

00:40:41.710 --> 00:40:43.860
and the whole revolution
of machine learning

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:47.700
is you put your answers
in, you put your data in,

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:48.974
and you get your rules out.

00:40:48.974 --> 00:40:50.640
And as a programmer,
if you're switching

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:52.770
that pivot from
what you've done,

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:55.730
it opens up these massive,
massive new scenarios for you

00:40:55.730 --> 00:40:58.620
that you can provide value for
yourself, for your business,

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:00.192
and for the world at large.

00:41:00.192 --> 00:41:01.650
So that's our goal
with TensorFlow.

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:03.941
And that's what we've been
working on in the last year.

00:41:03.941 --> 00:41:06.780
Our goal ultimately is to
make machines intelligent

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:08.190
and to improve people's lives.

00:41:08.190 --> 00:41:12.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

